    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Techniques for handling the effects of sorption in a simulation model were developed. The techniques account for the thermodynamics associated with adsorption and desorption. Also incorporated in the approach is a new matrix-fracture transfer function for a dual-porosity model of naturally fractured reservoirs. The combined effect of dual-porosity transfer and sorption was investigated.During the model construction process, a simple and convenient approach for constructing the residual and Jacobian arrays in a reservoir simulator was developed. The key idea was to keep track of all the `connections' in a model, as opposed to the conventional approach of keeping track of gridblocks and wells, when computing the flux terms. This method made the implementation of features such as nonneighbor connection and inter-porosity flow extremely simple and without modification to the main computational algorithm. This approach can be extended in a natural way to include node connections in wells and surface flow line networks.The fundamental theory of matrix-fracture transfer for dual-porosity simulation was reviewed. New matrix-fracture transfer functions, which are physically and mathematically consistent, were derived by incorporating analytical solutions of pressure diffusion for various flow geometries. The resulting equations describing the matrix-fracture flow for one, two and three parallel sets of fractures were obtained without making the pseudosteady state assumption. A generalized matrix-fracture transfer function for anisotropic, rectangular matrix blocks was also obtained. The transfer functions were verified by matching results from fine grid single-porosity and one block dual-porosity models. The transfer functions derived in this study are consistent with observations cited in the literature on dual-porosity simulation models.The model for investigating the effect of sorption was verified by matching data from several laboratory experiments. The results indicated that simulation runs using sorption isotherms adequately modeled the pressure transient behavior observed in the laboratory experiments. Dry steam models severely underestimated the effective compressibility. Models using at interface (steam table) thermodynamics overestimated the compressibility of the system, indicated by a slower than actual rate of pressure transient propagation.Results from models combining dual-porosity matrix-fracture transfer and the effects of sorption were also analyzed. The results indicated that the matrix-fracture transfer relationships derived in this study are also applicable in the presence of an adsorbed phase. Field scale models were used to illustrate the application of the dual-porosity and sorption models. Depletion and water injection problems were investigated. The results indicated that the effects of sorption resulted in significantly different saturation distributions compared to those from the flat-interface model.",
